  So "shared across the app" has no existing seam to hang off. There's also no test runner and no linter configured.
  I'll fold the module-format question into the design, but first the decision that constrains everything else.

  [1mThe fork: where tracked events actually live.[0m This is the expensive one to change later — it sets the storage format,
  whether you need a backend, and what the module's API looks like (fire-and-forget vs. async-and-fallible).

  - [1mBrowser storage ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys you persistence across reloads with zero backend work, and the module stays
    synchronous and easy to call from any form. Costs: the data is per-device and per-browser, you can't see it
    server-side, there's a ~5MB ceiling you eventually have to prune, and anything stored is readable by any script on
    the page — which matters because login events are identity data.
  - [1mSend to a backend[0m — real tracking you can actually query across users and devices. Costs: [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub, so
    this needs an endpoint that doesn't exist yet; the module becomes async and has to define what happens when the
    request fails or the user is offline. Rules out treating tracking as fire-and-forget.
  - [1mBuffer locally, flush to backend[0m — the robust answer, and what production analytics does: survives offline, no lost
    events. Costs: the most machinery by a wide margin — queue, flush trigger, retry, de-duplication. Hard to justify
    before you have an endpoint at all.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser storage now, with the module's API written so the storage backend can be swapped without [0m
  [1mtouching call sites.[0m You don't have a backend endpoint yet, and the "other forms will need it later" requirement is
  really about the [3mcalling interface[0m being stable — that's the part worth designing carefully. Getting the interface
  right makes the storage choice cheap to revisit; getting it wrong doesn't.
